    howItWorks:
      "Vík í Mýrdal is a village on the south coast of Iceland in Mýrdalshreppur municipality with about 750 inhabitants as of 2021, the southernmost settlement in the country; Iceland has only the state and municipalities, the municipality running schools, planning and local services while the state runs health care and police. Its function is service: along some 70 km of coast between Skógar and Mýrdalssandur this is the only place with fuel, a supermarket and hotels, so nearly everyone on the Ring Road stops here once. The black sand beach and the Reynisdrangar stacks offshore are its famous sight, listed among the ten most beautiful beaches on earth by an American magazine in 1991; but the surf here throws sudden sneaker waves far up the sand, warning signs stand along it, and people are swept out every year. It is the wettest coastal town in Iceland with about 2,250 mm of rain a year. North of the village the Katla volcano lies beneath the Mýrdalsjökull ice cap and last erupted in 1918; an eruption would send meltwater down in very little time, and the church on the hill is the village assembly point. Newcomers most often go wrong by turning their back to the sea for a photograph, which is exactly how people are taken.",
